  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1058 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nA pleasant overnight period is currently progressing across the\nMid-South with some cloudiness generally across West TN.\nTemperatures across the region are currently in the 60s to low\n70s, which is certainly welcome after the past month and a half.\nAs far as records go, Memphis did \"only\" reach 89 degrees today,\nwhich means our streak of consecutive 90 degree days officially\ncame to an end with 49 days in a row. Into Wednesday, an upper-\nlevel ridge will continue to build just to our west, keeping the\nMid-South in a northwesterly flow regime. This pattern will allow\nwarm and dry conditions to develop through the remainder of the\nweek. While high temperatures are forecast to remain in the mid\nto upper 80s, maybe with an occasional 90 degree reading in\nMississippi, this will still be a few degrees above normal for\nthe end of September.\n\nOver the upcoming weekend, the aforementioned upper-level ridge\nwill finally begin to break down, allowing for a series of\nshortwaves to pass through. While confidence is low in overall\nprecipitation coverage, isolated shower and thunderstorm chances\nwill begin to creep back into the forecast by Sunday and into the\nearly portions of next week. Through this same time frame, high\ntemperatures will continue to nudge upward a few degrees,\ngenerally back into the upper 80s to low 90s. As we look towards\nthe end of the current forecast period, guidance is beginning to\nhint at a strong upper-level system moving through the region\nsomewhere towards the middle of next week. While uncertainty\nremains, this appears to be one of the stronger systems we have\nseen and could finally usher in true, fall-like conditions. There\nis still plenty of time and uncertainty, but some true fall-like\nweather could be on the horizon.",
